<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hopewell Culture National Historical Park</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mound City Group National Monument, Mound City Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hopewell Culture National Historical Park is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> national historical park in Ross County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, that preserves earthworks and burial mounds of the Hopewell culture, which flourished from about 200 BCE to 500 CE. It began as the Mound City Group, much of it destroyed by an army training camp built on the site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, which was declared a National Monument in 1923; in 1992 it was renamed and expanded to include other earthwork and mound groups, among them the Hopewell Mound Group, the culture's type site, and the geometric Seip and Hopeton Earthworks. It is run by the National Park Service and became part of the Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hopewell-culture-national-historical-park.jpg" alt="Hopewell Culture National Historical Park" width="330" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ɱ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hopewell_Culture_National_Historical_Park.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The park's sites lie in the Scioto River valley near Chillicothe, where the visible remains of the Hopewell culture are concentrated. Mound City Group, on State Route 104 about 4 miles north of Chillicothe, holds the visitor centre and is the only fully restored Hopewell site: its mounds stand inside a roughly square earthen wall 1.2 metres high. Each mound covered the remains of a charnel house, burned after the dead had been cremated, with offerings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> figures, mica, projectile points, shells and pipes.</p><p>The Seip Earthworks, 17 miles west of Chillicothe near Bainbridge, join a large circle 494 metres across, a smaller circle and a square. The Hopeton Earthworks, across the river from Mound City, include a circle 320 metres across, a square and a pair of parallel earthen lines that follow the sun's path at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>. Spruce Hill, above Paint Creek, is enclosed by a stone wall, and High Bank Works, a circle and octagon each about 1,000 feet across, is closed to the public.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The builders worked to a monumental scale, raising walls up to 12 feet high around geometric figures and conical mounds up to 30 feet high. They combined different soils for stability and carried the earth in handwoven baskets. The Hopewell Mound Group covers 300 acres and holds 29 mounds; its largest, three joined circles 500 feet long, is the largest known Hopewell mound. The culture is named after a former owner of this land, where an excavation took place in 1891.</p><p>European Americans first mapped Mound City in the 1840s. Its first excavators, Ephraim George Squier and Edwin Hamilton Davis, gathered a large collection of artefacts now held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. The United States Army built Camp Sherman over the site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and razed it after the war, and the Ohio Historical Society excavated the mounds from 1920 to 1922 and rebuilt them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The park is administered by the National Park Service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Interior. Its visitor centre at Mound City shows an orientation film and offers self-guided tours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> mound complexes once recorded in Ross County have since been lost to agriculture or urban development.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hopewell Culture National Historical Park” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Hopewell Culture National Historical Park</b> near Chillicothe, Ohio, protects the mounds that gave the culture its name, and the Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks are a UNESCO World Heritage Site preserving Ohio Hopewell sites.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
